# Bake config: a mobile-CI macOS image for your-org/your-app - fastlane,
# cocoapods, jq, Watchman, xcpretty, Node, the Android SDK (compileSdk 36 +
# NDK/CMake), and Maestro, on top of the cirruslabs Xcode image. Bake with:
#   ./bake-image.sh -d mobile-bake runner.example.com

# Base image to build on. The -xcode images ship Xcode + Homebrew; use
# macos-tahoe-base for a minimal starting point.
#
# Pinned by digest so a re-bake starts from the same bytes: `:latest` moved
# under v5 (which cannot be rebuilt as it was) and the tags themselves are
# mutable. Tart takes a digest OR a tag, not both, so the tag is a comment.
# This is macos-tahoe-xcode:26.5 (Xcode 26.5 / 17F42 on macOS 26.4) as of
# 2026-09-02, what the 18/18-green E2E suite ran on. To move: pick a tag from
#   curl -s "https://ghcr.io/v2/cirruslabs/macos-tahoe-xcode/tags/list" \
#     -H "Authorization: Bearer $(curl -s 'https://ghcr.io/token?scope=repository:cirruslabs/macos-tahoe-xcode:pull' | jq -r .token)"
# then read its digest from the docker-content-digest header of
#   curl -sI ".../manifests/<tag>" with the same bearer token and
#   -H "Accept: application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json"
# and bump BAKE_IMAGE_NAME.
BAKE_BASE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/cirruslabs/macos-tahoe-xcode@sha256:61f6e857a3d65dd2f8daf9c51c7b837fa458bcc9181ae8556e645b534dab6bf6

# Local name of the baked image (no '/'). Version it: bump the suffix when
# you change the scripts, then update MACVM_IMAGE and re-provision.
# v2 adds the gcloud CLI (required by the yarn gcp-auth plugin for the
# private @your-company packages) and the Cloudflare WARP client (unregistered;
# each VM enrols at boot for VPN access during GraphQL codegen).
# v3 was v2 plus a separate prime layer, so it was not reproducible in one
# pass; v4 installs gcloud from Google's own tarball instead of the Homebrew
# cask (whose post-install pip-fetched a wheel from github.com and purged
# itself when that failed) and runs the deep prime inline, so a single clean
# bake from the base image yields toolchain AND warm caches again. v5 adds
# Watchman, required by the mobile-apps `yarn e2e` preflight. v6 adds ccache
# (for MACVM_KEEP_CACHES + USE_CCACHE=1) and pins what v5 left floating: the
# base image (digest above) and the Maestro CLI (10-mobile-tooling.sh), so
# it is the first image that can be re-baked as it was. v7 turns the Android
# prime into a real release build so the compile toolchain and artifact
# transforms are warm - v6 jobs fetched ~700MB more and redid every transform
# - prunes stale Gradle distributions, warms the patient app only, and
# switches Gradle's build cache on in the image. v8 installs
# the same versions as v7 but reads every one of them from the repo's
# versions.env instead of literals in the scripts, asserts what it installed
# against that file, and prints a "pin check" after the warm build comparing
# it with what the build actually used; `mise run runner:pins` does the same
# against mobile-apps from here. The persistent share already carries the
# dependency and build caches on the runner host, so bake v8 with the next version
# move rather than for its own sake. v9 is v8 on a 180 GB disk (see
# BAKE_DISK_SIZE_GB below). v10 is the eMudhra CA key compromise response
# (Oct 2026): it drops the two affected roots from /etc/ssl/cert.pem
# (05-distrust-emudhra.sh), picks up Homebrew's fixed ca-certificates, Node
# 22.23.4 and WARP 2026.8.2115.0, and points gcloud at Homebrew's CA store
# instead of its vendored certifi. It also syncs versions.env with mobile-apps
# (Expo SDK 57, React Native 0.86.3, Gradle 9.3.1), so the prime's pin check
# reads clean again.
BAKE_IMAGE_NAME=ghrunner-mobile-v10

# Guest disk size in GB (unset keeps the base image's 140 GB). Every job VM is
# a clone of this image, so this is each job's disk. At 140 GB the baked
# toolchain and caches left ~19 GB free at job start, and the expo57 Maestro
# suite filled it on 2026-10-05: the runner itself died with ENOSPC and the job
# lost its logs and artifacts. The disk is sparse and clones are copy-on-write,
# so the host pays only for what jobs write, but two slots can each write up to
# ~40 GB more than before: keep that much free on the host.
BAKE_DISK_SIZE_GB=180

# Resources for the VM while baking. `yarn install` across ~150 private
# packages plus two `pod install`s is the bulk of the work.
BAKE_CPUS=4
BAKE_MEMORY_MB=8192
# Hard controller-side limit for each scripts/*.sh invocation. This bounds a
# live-but-stuck SSH channel as well as bugs in a guest script; on expiry the
# bake fails, its temporary VM is deleted, and paused runner slots resume.
BAKE_SCRIPT_TIMEOUT_MIN=120

# Guest credentials (cirruslabs images ship admin/admin).
#BAKE_SSH_USER=admin
#BAKE_SSH_PASSWORD=admin

# Deep cache warming (20-prime-repo.sh). Any BAKE_PRIME_* var is forwarded
# into the guest scripts. Without them the deep prime self-skips and only the
# no-auth Gradle prewarm (15-prime-tools.sh) runs - which is how v2 shipped
# with a COLD pod cache and spent ~7m22s of every iOS job re-downloading all
# 171 pods. Treat both tokens as mandatory:
#   BAKE_PRIME_TOKEN     - GitHub token, read access to the app repo (clone)
#   BAKE_PRIME_GCP_TOKEN - GCP access token for Artifact Registry. The private
#                          @your-company packages need it, and a failed `yarn
#                          install` leaves no node_modules for CocoaPods to
#                          autolink against, so the prime stops before
#                          `pod install` ever runs.
# Access tokens last ~1h; mint both immediately before baking.
#
# DO NOT COMMIT REAL TOKENS - export them in the shell instead. bake-image.sh
# injects any BAKE_PRIME_* from the environment into the payload's copy of this
# file, which is deleted after the bake:
#   BAKE_PRIME_TOKEN=$(gh auth token) \
#   BAKE_PRIME_GCP_TOKEN=$(gcloud auth print-access-token) \
#     ./bake-image.sh -d mobile-bake -b runner.example.com
#
# `yarn build` (GraphQL codegen) would fail here: it introspects the VPN-only
# api.internal.example.com and a bake guest has no WARP enrolment. Pod warming
# does not depend on it - `expo prebuild` generates the native project from app
# config, and `pod install` needs node_modules, not generated types. Codegen is
# therefore skipped by default. Set this only when the bake guest has a verified
# private route; normal runner VMs enroll into WARP separately at boot.
BAKE_PRIME_WITH_CODEGEN=0
#
# Check the tail of /var/log/ghrunner-bake.log for the "cache summary" the
# prime prints. Empty or MISSING there means the warm silently did not happen,
# which is how v2 shipped cold in the first place.
#BAKE_PRIME_TOKEN=
#BAKE_PRIME_GCP_TOKEN=
#BAKE_PRIME_REPO=your-org/your-app
#BAKE_PRIME_REF=main
# Apps to warm (space-separated <dir> names). The Android warm is a full
# release build per app (~12 min at BAKE_CPUS=4), so only the app these runners
# build. Add provider-app when it comes back.
#BAKE_PRIME_APPS=patient-app
# Minutes before the Android warm build is killed. The v7 bake hung for ten
# hours in a Gradle --continue scheduler deadlock with the macvm slot paused,
# and the v9 bake (2026-10-05) hung the same way ~13 min in, after FAILED :app
# resource tasks and while still compiling. The cold warm build took ~11 min
# at BAKE_CPUS=4 in v7 and runs longer now. Forwarded like every BAKE_PRIME_*
# var; a kill degrades to a warning, the caches so far are kept.
#BAKE_PRIME_GRADLE_TIMEOUT_MIN=25
